      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study aims to contribute to a better understanding of education for AI by focusing on students' conceptual understanding of related concepts. We utilized the scientific argumentation framework to help develop students' conceptual understanding, as literature has shown that mere exposure to the framework procures conceptual change [54]. In this study, we used this framework not only to structure student learning but also to shape our research objectives, analyze results, and draw conclusions from our work to the broader field. To sketch the process of scientific reasoning, experiences gained in the natural world are considered data points that coalesce into patterns of phenomena. Then, simply, hypotheses and theories are created based on these patterns of phenomena. Experiments are then run to test those hypotheses and theories. Now, a scientific argument formulates the reasoning process further. Formulating the reasoning process further, a scientific argument captures how claims and explanations of phenomena are justified and supported by evidence [15]. In this study, the theory of scientific argumentation is articulated into a framework where the scientific thinking and reasoning process can be examined as artifacts of students' learning [21]. \n\nInitially constructed by Stephen E. Toulmin in 1958 [50], an argument assesses the relationship between a claim and support for such a claim [3,50]. As it has been widely influential in science education, multiple iterations and adaptations of Toulmin's work explore different uses for argumentation [3]. For instance, Jimenez-Aleixandre et al. [19] used scientific argumentation as a basis to characterize whether students simply completed science lessons \"for school\" or whether they were interested in the science behind the lesson. Moreover, to capture students' quality of argument in dialog with peers, Osborne et al. [30] used the framework to distinguish levels of complexity in students' argumentation. Other approaches to assessing arguments have converged with Toulmin's scientific argumentation framework, as well. Duschl [10] reviewed competing frameworks that delineate how knowledge of the claim and the claim itself interact, as well as implications for examining how evidence is applicable or justified for the claim. Nevertheless, these different approaches and frameworks correspond by their reference to claims and various degrees of support for those claims.",
