      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial Intelligence (AI) refers to the simulation of human intelligence by machines, enabling interaction with humans to influence behavior and assist in accomplishing tasks beyond individual capabilities (Chintalapati & Pandey, 2022;Holmes & Tuomi, 2022;Uzir et al., 2023). AI in education is called AIEd (Mello et al., 2023;Schiff, 2021), and refers to technology designed to support teachers by automating routine tasks, providing actionable insights, and enhancing instructional planning (Feuerriegel et al., 2024;Kyrpa et al., 2024;Velander et al., 2024). In the 1960s, ELIZA was one of the first attempts to simulate human-like conversation using simple pattern-matching techniques. ELIZA's primary function was to engage users in dialogue, mimicking a therapeutic conversation (Al-Amin et al., 2024). This early AI system laid the foundation for more advanced AIEd technologies by demonstrating how AI could facilitate learning through interaction. Over the decades, AI has evolved from basic systems to advanced applications, such as generative AI, which combines machine learning and natural language processing to power tools like ChatGPT, Canva, Eduaide, Gamma, and Gemini, transforming the way complex educational tasks are handled (Feuerriegel et al., 2024;Kyrpa et al., 2024;Velander et al., 2024). \n\nAIEd plays many roles and has many advantages, such as improving teacher productivity, offering support, and resolving issues (Bai et al., 2021;Cukurova et al., 2023;Wang et al., 2021). Moreover, it includes tailoring learning experiences for students, streamlining assessment processes, and utilizing predictive analytics (Mello et al., 2023;Velander et al., 2024). It reduces administrative burdens by managing student records, tracking attendance, and handling other logistical tasks, freeing teachers to focus on their core responsibilities (Feuerriegel et al., 2024;Kyrpa et al., 2024;Velander et al., 2024). AIEd tasks involve analyzing student data, creating learning environments, and integrating resources (C.",
