      "snippet": {
        "text": "Our design builds on established frameworks for expert problem solving and Socratic inquiry. Adams and Wieman's multi-skill model identifies conceptual understanding, strategic planning, and self-monitoring as core sub-skills in physics problem solving [21,22]. Epistemic-game theory further maps the cognitive moves experts use to structure solutions [6], while multiple-representation studies highlight the importance of fluid transitions among algebraic, graphical, and pictorial modes [5]. Rubric-based assessments provide reliable snapshots of written work [3,7], and metacognitive post-reflection exercises yield measurable gains in strategic reasoning [3,8]. \n\nSocratic tutoring, which uses targeted questions to elicit self-explanations, has long been shown to enhance learning by revealing and correcting misconceptions [10,22]. Intelligent tutoring systems that implement Socratic dialogue achieve substantial learning gains in physics and other domains [9]. More recently, PER has begun to explore AI chatbots: Dunlap et al. demonstrated Large Language Model (LLM) performance on kinematics graphs [18], and Polverini et al. evaluated ChatGPT on the BEMA inventory and concluded that ChatGPT exhibits significant difficulties in engaging with physics tasks involving visual representations. [15]. Fussell et al. compared LLMs for lab-note analysis [13], and Chen & Wan showed human-level feedback generation on problem-solving explanations [12]. Kieser et al. applied data augmentation with ChatGPT to train analytic models [14], and Kortemeyer et al. explored AI grading feasibility on handwritten exams [17,19]. \n\nDespite these advances, transcript-based dialogue analytics remain scarce. Existing studies tend to focus on performance metrics or teacher training, rather than authentic student-bot interactions parsed for question specificity and linked directly to learning outcomes [11,20]. By integrating Socratic scaffolding with automated analytics on complete 