      "snippet": {
        "text": "There are significant differences in the materials and teaching strategies that are most effective for different age groups, categories, learning environments, and expected learning objectives. Their research confirms that significant progress has been made in the promotion of AI literacy education over the past two decades (Jatileni et al., 2023;Yim & Su, 2024). This emphasizes the appropriateness of employing intelligent agents, including Google's Teachable Machine (GTM) and Machine Learning for Kids (MLKs), as age-suitable tools for AI literacy education in K-12 settings (George et al., 2023). \n\nAI education is acknowledged as a catalyst for economic growth, future workforce development, and global competitiveness (Cetindamar et al., 2022;Sestino & De Mauro, 2022). The effort to equip students/learners and educators with AI knowledge, skills, and attitudes to thrive in an AI-dominated future has encouraged the emergence of the term \"AI literacy\" (Wang & Cheng, 2021), which pertains to the conception and implementation of AI learning activities, tools and applications, and pedagogical models in the K-12 context (Dai et al., 2024;Miao et al., 2021;Rina et al., 2022). Consequently, research on human-AI interaction has assumed greater significance in diverse fields where AI tools are extensively employed, such as autonomous driving (AD), healthcare, robotics, and education (Cetindamar et al., 2022;Dai et al., 2024;Jatileni et al., 2023). AI presents a vast array of applications that possess the capability to provide substantial benefits, including but not limited to intelligent tutoring and automated grading systems in the K-12 context. AI gives innovative educational prospects for both learning and teaching objectives (George et al., 2023;Sanusi, Ayanwale, & Chiu, 2024). One such promising opportunity is the use of AI-driven tools which hold the potential to foster a learner-centred approach (Celik, 2023).",
