      "snippet": {
        "text": "The process of implementing CyberScholar in schools began by integrating teaching materials, writing prompts, and rubrics from the teachers through fine-tuning. This is done through Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) technology. i. e., materials are added to CyberScholar to supplement the information available in the underlying GenAI foundation model used in the platform. This ensures that feedback is aligned with educational standards and teacher expectations. Also, the materials were processed through AI prompt engineering to align feedback with classroom expectations. This is the main difference of the use of AI being evaluated by this research; it is used pedagogically, considering teachers' work, materials being used in class, and rubrics. \n\nThe initial trials were conducted in four public schools in the United States: three high schools and one middle school. The first, referred to as School A, is a public school located in an underserved area in the Midwest region of the country. The second, School B, is a well-resourced university laboratory high school, also in the Midwest. The third, School C, is a rural, mid-poverty middle school located in the Northwest. The fourth, School D, is a mid-poverty high school, also in the Midwest. These schools represent a diverse range of socioeconomic contexts: School A serves students from an underserved community with varied backgrounds, Schools C and D are situated in mid-poverty areas, and School B admits students from highly privileged social and economic backgrounds. \n\nSchool A has 824 students enrolled in the 2024 school year, spanning grades 9 through 12. Its demographics show that approximately 35% of the student population is White, 30% is Hispanic, and 24% is Black. The highest enrollment is in Grade 9 (n=233) and Grade 10 (n=235), with a decline in Grade 11 (n=184) and Grade 12 (n=175). The teacher-student ratio is 13 to 1. The total dropout rate is 2.8%, with the highest incidence among Hispanic students. Regarding socioeconomic status, 99.6% of students qualify as low-income, meaning they are eligible for free or reduced-price lunches, live in substitute care, or belong to families receiving public aid (Illinois Report Card, 2023).",
