      "snippet": {
        "text": "The integration of AI technologies in education has become increasingly prominent, with the appearance of AI chatbots like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Bing AI. These chatbots have great potential to revolutionize teachers' teaching practices. For example, since its first public release on November 30, 2022, ChatGPT has amazed the world with its extraordinary ability to complete complex tasks (Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023). The strength of ChatGPT lies in its ability to interact through natural conversation, which involves a series of questions from the user and responses from the application (Rospigliosi, 2023). ChatGPT is able to generate coherent, partially correct, and systematic responses and then retain the topic and history of the conversation (Houston & Corrado, 2023;Zhai, 2022). With impressive achievements, ChatGPT has successfully passed exams of prestigious organizations such as the University of Minnesota law exam (Kelly, 2023), the United States Medical Licensing Examination (Abdel-Messih & Kamel Boulos, 2023;Subramani et al., 2023), and standardized tests in the United Kingdom (Giannos & Delardas, 2023), etc. It is not surprising that many schools and teachers have expressed concerns about the negative impact of ChatGPT on learners and the possibility of cheating on their assignments (Kelly, 2023). However, most educators advocate that ChatGPT should be used as a learning tool rather than fearing its negative impacts (Ali et al., 2023;Eager & Brunton, 2023). The presence of AI systems and chatbots in education should be seen as an opportunity for development rather than a threat (Kooli, 2023). \n\nIn the fierce race for AI Chatbots, Google launched Bard AI (later renamed Gemini) on February 6, 2023, and shortly after, on February 8, 2023, Microsoft officially launched Bing Chat. These chatbots can respond and interact naturally with users, similar to ChatGPT (Rudolph et al., 2023).",
