        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Epley  and colleagues (2007) describe mechanisms that are particularly relevant to conversational AI. Social motivation explains that when social needs are high, they project a mind onto responsive agents to fulfill a desire for connection 7 . Currently, conversational generative AI chatbots strongly promote anthropomorphism because they generate human like language using large language models, respond contingently and present as socially responsive. This makes it easy for users to feel understood and to experience the interaction as social. Indeed, recent studies report that adult users of relational chatbots report that conversations reduce loneliness because they feel heard 8 , and a longitudinal study showed that one week of interaction with a relational conversational style chatbot led to stronger perceived social bonds with the chatbot compared to a non-relational conversational style 9 . This social motivation mechanism may be especially salient in early adolescence. Young adolescents are still developing social cognition and mind perception, are highly sensitive to peer evaluation and to difficulties in peer and family relationships 10,11 . Adolescents and young adults who engage in socially supportive conversations with chatbots tend to report greater loneliness and lower perceived social support than non-users 12 . Age related differences in AI literacy may further shape anthropomorphism, with younger adolescents more likely to attribute human qualities to chatbots than older adolescents 13,14 . Recent work with large language model chatbots suggests that younger adolescents, particularly under age 15, can show high levels of anthropomorphism, sometimes describing AI chatbots as \"friends\" similar to their human friends 15 . This combination of developmental sensitivity to social feedback, relatively low AI literacy and pervasive access to highly human like chatbots raises the possibility that relational conversational styles may elicit strong anthropomorphism and, in turn, emotional reliance in young adolescence. \n\nThe conversational style is a key design mechanism that can modulate the emotional dependence tendencies. Relational style refers to conversational cues that signal warmth and social presence, including first person voice, affiliative, relationship-oriented reflection, phatic small talk, and commitment language such as \"I am here for you.\" These cues increase perceived social presence, perceived empathy, trust, enjoyment and felt support, and can make interactions feel nonjudgmental and private for adolescents.",
