      "snippet": {
        "text": "Moreover, it cultivates pivotal competencies for socioscientific reasoning, systems thinking, and informal decision-making (Demir & Namdar, 2021;Ke et al., 2021b). Pairing SSI with scientific modeling provides an opportunity for students to apply their scientific knowledge in a real-world context (Ke et al., 2021b). Model-based learning (MbL) further enhances SSI-based instruction by encouraging students to develop, test, and refine models collaboratively, thereby mirroring scientific processes (Shen et al., 2014). This approach is conducive to diverse learning styles, fosters peer collaboration, and provides opportunities for alternative reasoning. \n\nDespite these findings, there is limited guidance on how teachers can effectively integrate modeling into SSI instruction. The development of professional development (PD) programs that focus on this integration is critical for addressing this gap. These programs should not only prioritize the integration of SSI and modeling/MbL but also examine teachers' professional growth and engagement throughout the process. This study aims to address these needs by exploring how a PD program can support middle school science teachers in integrating SSI and modeling/MbL into their classrooms, ultimately advancing the goal of scientific literacy. The Interconnected Model of Professional Growth, or IMPG, (Clarke & Hollingsworth, 2002) framework guides this study to analyze science teachers' engagement and growth through the PD program. Accordingly, the research questions aligned with the IMPG domains are as follows: \n\n1. What is the nature of the middle school science teachers' beliefs, attitudes, and knowledge about science teaching and learning? (Personal Domain) 2. Which elements of socioscientific issue and model-based learning framework do the middle school science teachers implement in their classrooms? (Domain of Practice) 3. What do the middle school science teachers perceive as salient outcomes when they implement their socioscientific issue and model-based learning units? (Domain of Consequences) 4. How are the middle school science teachers' interactions with each other and the researcher in the PD program? (External Domain) 2 Background of the Study",
