          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12273424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is a category of AI that learns patterns from large datasets and uses these learned patterns to produce new content, including text, images, music, or videos. Large language models (LLMs) are a type of generative AI that forms the basis of chatbot tools, such as OpenAI's ChatGPT and Google's Gemini. Artificial intelligence has an emerging role in medicine to assist with education, clinical decision-making, and treatment planning. 1,2 Validating the accuracy of AI in these tasks is increasingly important for their responsible and reliable translation from scientific inquiry to clinical application. \n\nIn the United States, the field of ophthalmology relies on standardized examinations administered by the American Board of Ophthalmology to assure the public that practitioners have the medical knowledge, clinical judgment, and professionalism required to provide high-quality patient care. \n\nPrevious research has demonstrated that both ChatGPT and Gemini are capable of modest performance on ophthalmology boardestyle questions, with reported accuracies ranging from 46.7% to 71%. 3,4 Next-generation chatbots have been demonstrated to outperform these earlier models in nonophthalmic fields of medicine and in other areas of complex scientific reasoning. 5 For example, GPT-4 significantly outperformed ChatGPT in neurosurgery boardestyle questions, indicating that more advanced models can offer enhanced accuracy and reasoning abilities. 6 Reasoning models are LLMs designed for complex reasoning in advanced disciplines, including science and mathematics. However, there is a lack of literature evaluating the performance of reasoning models on board-style examinations, which could give insight into the progression of AI models and the future of AI in medical education and care. \n\nIn this study, we compared the performance of OpenAI o1, a novel LLM from OpenAI designed specifically for complex reasoning, to OpenAI's flagship LLM, GPT-4o, and its competitor, Google's Gemini 1.5 Flash, on ophthalmology board-style questions. We hypothesized that OpenAI o1 would outperform both GPT-4o and Gemini Flash 1.5 given advances in technology and its purpose-built nature.",
